[{:db/id "q-168866/field-1/f261e539246084db28473397dcabcd8c46bd0c6cec47308267e7750313016d8c" :answer/id #uuid "97a94aee-23c7-5db6-ba95-9e23b83c2865" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10y^2-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168866/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ae6ede1-3c3d-5479-8286-7f7c48c836b1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-168866/field-1/f261e539246084db28473397dcabcd8c46bd0c6cec47308267e7750313016d8c" :answer-field/choices ["q-168866/field-1/f261e539246084db28473397dcabcd8c46bd0c6cec47308267e7750313016d8c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-168866/field-2/f51c0fb5f7d824e8e6febe5f1432922a217294b6d32a3c3f6e23d77db2e8a1f2" :answer/id #uuid "a9a7a956-10cb-5cbe-a1de-f2478511ba5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12y^2-10x^2+20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168866/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "aceae580-15e3-50f1-89ba-d0cf93ed8891" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-168866/field-2/f51c0fb5f7d824e8e6febe5f1432922a217294b6d32a3c3f6e23d77db2e8a1f2" :answer-field/choices ["q-168866/field-2/f51c0fb5f7d824e8e6febe5f1432922a217294b6d32a3c3f6e23d77db2e8a1f2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-168866/field-3/4d065f3d795e5de09b6a5896c69965aa32d743b475737214d890dbf3979839b3" :answer/id #uuid "140514f1-e572-5c55-8f05-d39e495db2f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-20xy" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168866/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "327f9d17-537a-50b1-99d6-39ef5e7ac793" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-168866/field-3/4d065f3d795e5de09b6a5896c69965aa32d743b475737214d890dbf3979839b3" :answer-field/choices ["q-168866/field-3/4d065f3d795e5de09b6a5896c69965aa32d743b475737214d890dbf3979839b3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-168866" :question/id #uuid "8702f31b-52d3-55f1-9139-138dcd0e7841" :question/math-academy-id "q-168866" :question/problem "A company produces two types of products, type A and type B. The revenue function of the company, in thousands of dollars, is given by $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=-{y}^{4}-5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+10{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x={[\\begin{aligned}\\,x & y\\,\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ $x$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product A, and $y$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product B. The company can adjust the prices per unit for both types of products by adding or removing certain features.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4y^3-10x^2y+20y$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $[$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-10xy^2-4x$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y]$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/assets/23b3e3c6e8eeecb32b38e868114bd2967882c5542edcfbee7f31f6e4a26d22da.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Given a sketch of the graph of $z=f(x)$ above, which of the points ($A,$ $B,$ or $C$) is a local maximum of the function?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        A local maximum is the point \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        A                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe critical points of $f$ are$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,\\pm $ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\sqrt{5}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)$ and $($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n, 0$).$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the second partial derivatives of $f.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}=\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-10x{y}^{2}-4x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y.\n$$\n\nComputing the second partial derivatives, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-10x{y}^{2}-4x)=-10{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-10x{y}^{2}-4x)=-20xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y)=-12{y}^{2}-10{x}^{2}+20\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-168866/field-1" "q-168866/field-2" "q-168866/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ea31275-77bd-59c7-8e1d-96e2e4b252a4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-168866"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-168865/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "c237b7dc-f9ac-5d0f-b84c-c02be9487e23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168865/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e64e1506-7dbc-5180-bd74-d6a8994ced09" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-168865/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-168865/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-168865/field-2/219347bb4a6f51134d7f5aff5da33544f44afee934064621ebff9cf9e335ee59" :answer/id #uuid "7298a49f-ccc4-5f5f-9f6f-3f7e142699d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168865/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "7888bf8a-e521-5766-9e92-fd47b8faf624" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-168865/field-2/219347bb4a6f51134d7f5aff5da33544f44afee934064621ebff9cf9e335ee59" :answer-field/choices ["q-168865/field-2/219347bb4a6f51134d7f5aff5da33544f44afee934064621ebff9cf9e335ee59"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-168865/field-3/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "16fdedf1-f44d-5062-a8f2-d50d19fb006f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168865/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "5975c673-fc2a-5350-8c8b-96085fb10705" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-168865/field-3/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-168865/field-3/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-168865" :question/id #uuid "2878166c-6222-50f8-90d4-be5415e8122e" :question/math-academy-id "q-168865" :question/problem "A company produces two types of products, type A and type B. The revenue function of the company, in thousands of dollars, is given by $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=-{y}^{4}-5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+10{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x={[\\begin{aligned}\\,x & y\\,\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ $x$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product A, and $y$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product B. The company can adjust the prices per unit for both types of products by adding or removing certain features.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4y^3-10x^2y+20y$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $[$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-10xy^2-4x$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y]$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/assets/23b3e3c6e8eeecb32b38e868114bd2967882c5542edcfbee7f31f6e4a26d22da.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Given a sketch of the graph of $z=f(x)$ above, which of the points ($A,$ $B,$ or $C$) is a local maximum of the function?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        A local maximum is the point \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        A                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe critical points of $f$ are$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,\\pm $ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)$ and $($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n, 0$).$" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-10x{y}^{2}-4x & \\,-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-10x{y}^{2}-4x=0 \\\\ -4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-2x(5{y}^{2}+2)=0 \\\\ -2y(2{y}^{2}+5{x}^{2}-10)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5{y}^{2}+2\\ne 0,$ from the first equation of the system, we obtain that\n\n$$\nx=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2y(2{y}^{2}+5{x}^{2}-10) & =0 \\\\ -2y(2{y}^{2}+5(0)^{2}-10) & =0 \\\\ -4y({y}^{2}-5) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,0).$\n\nNote that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these points, they are also stationary points." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-168865/field-1" "q-168865/field-2" "q-168865/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045854fc-9cad-5005-a306-9550848e25df"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-168865"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-168868/field-1/22814de28a0b1b4f20d5edd62707d192b78050d457a70349795d30d86dfcd7fa" :answer/id #uuid "bde957b4-0247-592b-941b-88cb09d9cb2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value ">" :d