\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=-{y}^{4}-5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+10{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x={[\\begin{aligned}\\,x & y\\,\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ $x$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product A, and $y$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product B. The company can adjust the prices per unit for both types of products by adding or removing certain features.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4y^3-10x^2y+20y$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $[$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-10xy^2-4x$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y]$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/assets/23b3e3c6e8eeecb32b38e868114bd2967882c5542edcfbee7f31f6e4a26d22da.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Given a sketch of the graph of $z=f(x)$ above, which of the points ($A,$ $B,$ or $C$) is a local maximum of the function?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        A local maximum is the point \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        A                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe critical points of $f$ are$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,\\pm $ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\sqrt{5}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)$ and $($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n, 0$).$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFind the second partial derivatives of $f.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-10y^2-4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-12y^2-10x^2+20$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}=\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-20xy$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nEvaluate the Hessian determinant of $f$ at $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,0).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $H(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})\\,=$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2160$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,H(0,0)\\,=$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-80$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nClassify the critical points using the second partial derivatives test.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$ are local maximum since $\\,H(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$0\\,\\,$ and $\\,\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$0$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $(0,0)$ is a saddle point since $\\,\\,H(0,0)$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$0$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n* If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n  * if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$ \n\n  * if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n* If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n* If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nIn part 4, we found the critical points $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,0).$ Their corresponding Hessian determinants and the second partial derivatives at these points were computed in part 6.\n\nWith that in mind, let's classify our critical points:\n\n* $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$ are local maximum of $f$ since $H(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})=2\\,160>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})=-54<0.$\n\n* $(0,0)$ is a saddle point since $H(0,0)=-80<0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-168868/field-1" "q-168868/field-2" "q-168868/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-168868"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-168864/field-1/e544e9814a718cb8bf727207693bea4c5780d52f3d687d776eb5f163b399ae27" :answer/id #uuid "30e35172-4ca0-590e-aa3c-728674f05a16" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168864/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "cb4b5327-8c1e-5e63-9fdc-c35bc2e1e6af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-168864/field-1/e544e9814a718cb8bf727207693bea4c5780d52f3d687d776eb5f163b399ae27" :answer-field/choices ["q-168864/field-1/e544e9814a718cb8bf727207693bea4c5780d52f3d687d776eb5f163b399ae27"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-168864" :question/id #uuid "0faa166d-d182-5da3-97cc-a09b62ab6189" :question/math-academy-id "q-168864" :question/problem "A company produces two types of products, type A and type B. The revenue function of the company, in thousands of dollars, is given by $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=-{y}^{4}-5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+10{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x={[\\begin{aligned}\\,x & y\\,\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ $x$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product A, and $y$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product B. The company can adjust the prices per unit for both types of products by adding or removing certain features.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4y^3-10x^2y+20y$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $[$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-10xy^2-4x$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y]$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/assets/23b3e3c6e8eeecb32b38e868114bd2967882c5542edcfbee7f31f6e4a26d22da.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Given a sketch of the graph of $z=f(x)$ above, which of the points ($A,$ $B,$ or $C$) is a local maximum of the function?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        A local maximum is the point \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nAmong the given options, such neighborhoods exist for the point $A.$ Hence, $A$ is a local maximum .\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/assets/d19c97886c1449b414939269d9f9bf5d7ca8b418268ed5ef9b5940acaf29dbf7.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-168864/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-168864"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-168867/field-1/78a74d3a7d228872a40d87a080a1c9b03422e782e5c8b2059d2542b49fb5203b" :answer/id #uuid "450572cb-ecf4-589c-9fc8-161f74999933" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2160" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168867/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "de2d2ec9-09b0-59e5-965d-0079034d3d7a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-168867/field-1/78a74d3a7d228872a40d87a080a1c9b03422e782e5c8b2059d2542b49fb5203b" :answer-field/choices ["q-168867/field-1/78a74d3a7d228872a40d87a080a1c9b03422e782e5c8b2059d2542b49fb5203b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-168867/field-2/d338149de316d0a826ef04c7391f29eae0e091a7d89189c437f8804c0309b165" :answer/id #uuid "61600bcf-9f25-5133-8198-410c7ca7de35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-80" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168867/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "52e84c59-0ff5-50f9-b64a-fcfa4533e843" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-168867/field-2/d338149de316d0a826ef04c7391f29eae0e091a7d89189c437f8804c0309b165" :answer-field/choices ["q-168867/field-2/d338149de316d0a826ef04c7391f29eae0e091a7d89189c437f8804c0309b165"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-168867" :question/id #uuid "22e1b298-983c-58d1-9280-05cf101acd27" :question/math-academy-id "q-168867" :question/problem "A company produces two types of products, type A and type B. The revenue function of the company, in thousands of dollars, is given by $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=-{y}^{4}-5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+10{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x={[\\begin{aligned}\\,x & y\\,\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ $x$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product A, and $y$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product B. The company can adjust the prices per unit for both types of products by adding or removing certain features.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4y^3-10x^2y+20y$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $[$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-10xy^2-4x$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y]$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/assets/23b3e3c6e8eeecb32b38e868114bd2967882c5542edcfbee7f31f6e4a26d22da.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Given a sketch of the graph of $z=f(x)$ above, which of the points ($A,$ $B,$ or $C$) is a local maximum of the function?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        A local maximum is the point \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        A                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe critical points of $f$ are$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,\\pm $ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\sqrt{5}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)$ and $($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n, 0$).$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFind the second partial derivatives of $f.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-10y^2-4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-12y^2-10x^2+20$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}=\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-20xy$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nEvaluate the Hessian determinant of $f$ at $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,0).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $H(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})\\,=$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,H(0,0)\\,=$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We computed the second partial derivatives in part 5. So, the second derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-10{y}^{2}-4 & -20xy \\\\ -20xy & -12{y}^{2}-10{x}^{2}+20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n* Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})=[\\begin{aligned}-54 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -40\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})=(-54)\\cdot (-40)-0\\cdot 0=2\\,160.\n$$\n\n* Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=(-4)\\cdot 20-0\\cdot 0=-80.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-168867/field-1" "q-168867/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "037ff08b-3246-5eed-870b-b4951f319c96"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-168867"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-168862/field-1/32bd0448d3d0baf2255022772f1ba70ee811ec46b9f8aca069ad3f449a980ef6" :answer/id #uuid "e1cda81b-0f3b-5ddc-9e2d-0f726b2f7108" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4y^3-10x^2y+20y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168862/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "502c717a-d842-5e34-9bdc-c92c507605a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-168862/field-1/32bd0448d3d0baf2255022772f1ba70ee811ec46b9f8aca069ad3f449a980ef6" :answer-field/choices ["q-168862/field-1/32bd0448d3d0baf2255022772f1ba70ee811ec46b9f8aca069ad3f449a980ef6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-168862" :question/id #uuid "3d3ccd66-44cc-5858-a138-7ed8bb28ce89" :question/math-academy-id "q-168862" :question/problem "A company produces two types of products, type A and type B. The revenue function of the company, in thousands of dollars, is given by $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=-{y}^{4}-5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+10{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x={[\\begin{aligned}\\,x & y\\,\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ $x$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product A, and $y$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product B. The company can adjust the prices per unit for both types of products by adding or removing certain features.\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{4}-5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+10{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{4})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{4})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the constant multiple rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{4})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10{y}^{2}) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{4})-5{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})+10\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4{y}^{3}-5{x}^{2}\\cdot 2y+10\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-168862/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14cc6590-c2fd-5395-9481-0e5c0d9b8b8b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-168862"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-168863/field-1/408fe4c08b8ac18cb763d61ed41332ea2ebf8c88b8c53f0f02f7bdd282c5569f" :answer/id #uuid "6e7e9fe4-ba19-5f71-9ae0-b2f1d54f558e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10xy^2-4x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168863/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ca79d037-a0b6-5722-9434-642337786b8d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-168863/field-1/408fe4c08b8ac18cb763d61ed41332ea2ebf8c88b8c53f0f02f7bdd282c5569f" :answer-field/choices ["q-168863/field-1/408fe4c08b8ac18cb763d61ed41332ea2ebf8c88b8c53f0f02f7bdd282c5569f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-168863" :question/id #uuid "348adeb4-b04c-5633-ac26-62027737b33e" :question/math-academy-id "q-168863" :question/problem "A company produces two types of products, type A and type B. The revenue function of the company, in thousands of dollars, is given by $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=-{y}^{4}-5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+10{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x={[\\begin{aligned}\\,x & y\\,\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ $x$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product A, and $y$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product B. The company can adjust the prices per unit for both types of products by adding or removing certain features.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4y^3-10x^2y+20y$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $[$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y]$" :question/worked-solution "For our scalar function, we have that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={(∇f)}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEarlier, we found that \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y.\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{4}-5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+10{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+2)=-10x{y}^{2}-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,-10x{y}^{2}-4x\\, & \\,-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-168863/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d912b296-6fd0-57c2-84e8-ca0b56549756"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-168863"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
